  "image_name": "36907afa799b3704927ad92b12c7c769.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how drug release from a polymer mesh depends mainly on the ratio between mesh size and drug size, and how this changes over time. The blue network represents the material mesh, and the gray particles are drug molecules. When the mesh is larger than the drug, molecules move out easily, giving fast diffusion. When mesh size is roughly similar to drug size, release is slower because the particles are more hindered as they pass through the network. When the mesh is smaller than the drug, the molecules are effectively trapped, leading to immobilization.\n\nThe figure also shows that trapped drugs can still be released later if the material structure changes. Three mechanisms are highlighted: degradation of the network, deformation of the material, and swelling. Each of these increases transport pathways or enlarges the openings in the mesh, allowing previously immobilized molecules to escape. The overall message is that drug mobility in these materials is not fixed only by particle size, but by the evolving physical state of the network. In short, release behavior ranges from fast to blocked, and can be reactivated by structural changes in the carrier material.",
